  A complex number is the sum of a real number and an imaginary number.
  It takes the form:

    z  =  x  +  √(-1) * y                //  √(x) means "square root of x".

    where x is the real part of z:      Re (z)  =  x
    and y is the imaginary part of z:   Im (z)  =  y

  Let's imagine a Cartesian plane whose horizontal axis (abscissa) is the ensemble ℝ (real numbers) and whose vertical axis (ordinate)
  is the ensemble I (pure imaginary numbers). This plane is called the complex plane.

  Now imagine we plot z on this plane. It shows how any complex number can be regarded as a vector joining the origin of both axis and the
  point with coordinates {x = Re (z) , y = Im (z)}.

  This vector traces the diagonal of a rectangle with two sides = Re (z) and two sides = Im (z).
  The vector z splits this rectangle in two identical right triangles whose hypotenuse is the length (magnitude) of the vector.

  This hypotenuse is called the absolute value of the complex number z and is notated |z|  --  sometimes abs (z).

  The theorem of Pythagoras gives:

    |z|  =  √( Re (z)²  +  Im (z)² )

  /*
    Notice that if  Im (z) = 0  then  |z| = √(Re (z)²)  which results in ignoring z's sign. Which is often mistaken for the actual
    definition of a number's absolute value (it stops being valid once said number has an imaginary part, while the definition given by
    complex analysis applies to purely real numbers. This coincides with the fact that real numbers are a subset of complex numbers).
  */

  Let's call θ the angle formed by the vector z and the real (horizontal) axis.
  θ is called the argument of the complex number z and is notated arg (z).
  Since Re (z) is its adjacent side and since Im (z) is its opposite side:

    tan (θ)  =  Im (z) / Re (z)

  And thus:

    arg  (z)  =  arctan ( Im (z) / Re (z) )    or    arctan2 ( Im (z), Re (z) )

  /*
    atan2:
      Angles in radians are in the range [0 , 2π]. While arctan (x) produces values in the range [-π , π].
      The modulus is the same: 2π. But arctan (x) has an offset of -π (clockwise flat angle).
      When summing several complex arguments, this offset can make the maths needed to retrieve the modulus a bit tricky,
      or at least not very elegant (unnecessarily crowded).
      The function atan2 fixes this.

      Also if Re (z) = 0 the regular arctan function (atan, tan^-1) faces a division by zero.
      The function atan2 fixes this too.
  */
  
  We also know that  cos (θ)  =  adjacent / hypotenuse   and    sin (θ)   =  opposite / hypotenuse
  which means:      adjacent  =  cos (θ) * hypotenuse    and    opposite  =  sin (θ) * hypotenuse

  Remember that adjacent is Re (z), opposite is Im (z), hypotenuse is |z| and θ is arg (z).
  Also that  z  =  Re (z)  +  √(-1) * Im (z).

  Then:

    Re (z)  =  |z| * cos ( arg (z) )
    Im (z)  =  |z| * sin ( arg (z) )

  Then:

    z  =  |z| * [ cos ( arg (z) )  +  √(-1) * sin ( arg (z) ) ]

    This expression is called the polar form of the complex number z.

  /*
    Complex numbers can be regarded as vectors. Vectors also have a polar form.
    The notation:  v  =  ( r , ∠θ )
    means:  v is a vector of magnitude r and angle θ.
    If v is represented with the complex number z, then:  |z| = r  and  arg (z) = θ.
  */

  The absolute value |z| of z is sometimes also called "modulus" or "magnitude".
  The argument arg (z) of z is sometimes also called "phase".



