#2  Euler's formula
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_function

  Let  j = √(-1)  and  x  be a real number, then Euler's formula states that:

    exp (j * x)  =  cos (x)  +  j * sin (x)                   //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler's_formula

  By making use of the 2 following trigonometric identities:

    cos (-x)  =  cos (x)    and    sin (-x)  =  -sin (x)      //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_trigonometric_identities#Reflections,_shifts,_and_periodicity

  Euler's formula can be re-expressed as:

    exp (j * -x)  =  cos (-x)  +  j * sin (-x)  =  cos (x)  -  j * sin (x)

  Then [1]:

    exp (j * x)  -  exp (-j * x)  =  cos (x)  +  j * sin (x)  -  ( cos (x)  -  j * sin (x) )  =  2 * j * sin (x)

  And [2]:

    exp (j * x)  +  exp (-j * x)  =  cos (x)  +  j * sin (x)  +  ( cos (x)  -  j * sin (x) )  =  2 * cos (x)


  Solving [1] for sin (x) gives:

                 exp (j * x)  -  exp (-j * x)
    sin (x)  =  ------------------------------
                            2 * j

  Solving [2] for cos (x) gives:

                 exp (j * x)  +  exp (-j * x)
    cos (x)  =  ------------------------------    [3]
                              2
  
  Considering the identity:

    cos (θ)  =  sin (θ  +  π / 2)

  Equation [3] shows that any real sinusoid can be expressed as half the sum of two complex exponentials.
  This is interesting because the rules of exponentiation are a lot simpler than the rules of trigonometry.
  For instance, exp (x) is its own derivative (and thus anti-derivative). This makes processes such as integration or differentiation become
  very trivial, compared to the same processes applied to cos (x) or sin (x).

  Let    z1  =  exp (j * x) / 2    ,    z2  =  exp (-j * x) / 2    , then:
    
    z1  =  cos (x) / 2  +  j * +sin (x) / 2
    z2  =  cos (x) / 2  +  j * -sin (x) / 2

  Their respective real (Re) and imaginary (Im) parts are thus:

    Re (z1)  =  cos (x) / 2    ;    Im (z1)  =  +sin (x) / 2
    Re (z2)  =  cos (x) / 2    ;    Im (z2)  =  -sin (x) / 2

    =>  Re (z1)  =  Re (z2)    ;    Im (z1)  =  -Im (z2)

  z1 and z2 form a complex conjugate pair.                          //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_conjugate
  So any real sinusoid (equation [3]) can be expressed as the sum of two complex conjugates.       [a]


  //	Euler's formula and the polar form

    Let's consider a complex number z with argument arg (z) and absolute value |z|.

    According to Euler's formula:

      exp ( j * arg (z) )  =  cos ( arg (z) )  +  j * sin ( arg (z) )

    Recalling the polar form expression:

      z  =  |z| * [ cos ( arg (z) )  +  j * sin ( arg (z) ) ]

    Combining these two expressions leads to:

      z  =  |z| * exp ( j * arg (z) )

    I haven't found any documentation on this form (perhaps from not digging deep enough...) so let's call it the exponential form of the
    complex number z.

    This means that any number in the form:

      ζ  =  α * exp (j * θ)

    has an absolute value    |ζ|  =  α    and an argument    arg (ζ)  =  θ

    Then    Re (ζ)  =  α * cos (θ)    and    Im (ζ)  =  α * sin (θ)

    Now in the special case:    θ  =  ω * t
    where t is the time (in seconds) and ω is an angular frequency (in radians per second), the exponential form:

      ζ  =  α * exp (j * ω * t)       [4]

    is what is called a COMPLEX SINUSOID.
    It is also called an analytic signal as it has optimal qualities in order to study a system's response to pure sinusoids.
    Indeed comparing equations [3] and [4] reveals that if we express a real sinusoid in terms of complex exponentials, we end up with two
    complex sinusoids, one of which has a negative frequency; while a pure complex sinusoid has its amplitude and phase embedded in its
    constant factor and only involves positive frequencies.
    More on that right away.

