#3  Frequency, Amplitude and Phase
===============================================================================================
  
  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/cxjrs1adv9

  If t is the time (in seconds),  ƒ (t)  =  cos (t)  is a sinusoid of:

    Amplitude	  α  =  1
    Period 		  T  =  2 * π  seconds
    Frequency	  f  =  1 / (2 * π)  Hz
    Phase		  φ  =  0  rad.

  That is,  ƒ (t)  oscillates from -1 to +1 through a repetitive pattern of length = 2 * π with an offset = 0.

  Notice that  ƒ (t)  =  α * cos (2 * π * f * t  +  φ)
  Or, after defining the angular frequency  ω  =  2 * π * f  (rad. / s.):      //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angular_frequency

    ƒ (t)  =  α * cos (ω * t  +  φ)     [5]

  That is,  ƒ (t)  oscillates from -α to +α through a repetitive pattern of length = 1 / ω with an offset = φ.

  Note that for φ to be expressed in radians (that is as a ratio of the period T), the offset t0 (seconds) must be defined.
  t0 is the time it takes for ƒ (t) to reach the beginning of an oscillation cycle ( ƒ (t) = 1 ) for the first time.
  (This implies  t0 < T)

  Then:  ƒ (t)  =  α * cos (ω * (t - t0))
  And:       φ  =  ω * -t0

  /*
    Remember that  cos (θ)  =  sin (θ  +  π / 2).
    Which means that the relation between sin and cos is simply a constant horizontal offset.
    I chose  ƒ (t)  =  cos (t)  as the base sinusoid, but  ƒ (t)  =  sin (t)  would be just as valid.
    In such a case  ƒ (t)  =  cos (t)  would have a phase  φ  =  π / 2  rad.

    This is a pure matter of convention.
    Since converting one convention into the other is as trivial as an addition, which one we choose has few consequences.
    So if one convention simplifies the maths, why choose the other? Right?

    We're just about to call back Euler's formula. Choosing cos avoids having j in the denominator.
    I hope now it doesn't feel arbitrary anymore.
  */

  Using equation [3] to express ƒ (t) in terms of complex exponentials instead of raw trigonometry:

               α                                   α
    ƒ (t)  =  --- * exp ( j * (ω * t  +  φ) )  +  --- * exp ( -j * (ω * t  +  φ) )
               2                                   2


               α * exp (j * φ)                            α * exp (j * -φ)        
    ƒ (t)  =  -----------------  * exp (j * ω * t)   +   ------------------  * exp (j * -ω * t)       [6]
                     2                                           2

  Comparing this expression of ƒ (t) to equation [4] shows that a real sinusoid is the sum of two complex sinusoids, one of which
  has a negative frequency.

  Let's define:

                                     α * exp (j * φ)
    s  =  j * ω    and    X (s)  =  -----------------
                                            2

  X (s) is a complex number in exponential form with absolute value  |X (s)|  =  α / 2  and argument  arg (X (s))  =  φ

  Then any complex sinusoid in the form:

    ƒ (t)  =  X (s) * exp (s * t)        //  where s is a *pure imaginary* number! (Re (s) = 0)

  can be considered the positive-frequency-component of a real sinusoid with:          [b]

    Amplitude	  α  =  2 * |X (s)|
    Phase		  φ  =  arg (X (s))
    Frequency	  ω  =  s / j  rad/s    or    f  =  s / (2 * π * j)  Hz

  /*
    X (s) is called the complex amplitude of ƒ (t). It embeds the actual amplitude and the phase.
    X (s) is just a symbol. The complex sinusoid  A * exp (z * t)  has a complex amplitude = A as long as  Re (z) = 0.
  */

  And thus the two (positive and negative) complex sinusoids that form a real sinusoid have equal amplitude and opposite phase.
  (Opposite phase because we measure it in radians, in fact they have the same t0.)

  /*
    Infinite feedback loop:
      # A real sinusoid is a sum of complex sinusoids.
      # A complex sinusoid is the sum of a real and an imaginary sinusoid.
  */

  There is a process called Hilbert transform which applies a different phase shift (± π/2) depending on whether the frequency
  is positive or negative. This ends up in doubling the amplitude of positive frequencies and cancelling out negative frequencies.
  Then real sinusoids have become complex sinusoids.
  (Not that simple if you stop sticking to cos, sin and exp.)

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert_transform

  A very convenient tool indeed. But I'd rather find a way to study signals without transforming them (even though it's easily reversible).
  So I'll take a different route.



