#6  Not so fast...
===============================================================================================

  D.C. offset needs closure.
  Remember "if you plot ƒ (t) it will shift the curve of the whole spectrum up or down by its value"?

  This statement contains the solution to the aporia: "How to deduce the D.C. offset from the Inverse Fourier Transform?".

  A spectrum is a sum of sinusoids. So the vertical shift of a spectrum is a vertical shift of sinusoid(s).
  A sinusoid without a D.C. offset has a period: a unique motif repeated identically ad infinitum.
  This period has a center of symmetry in its middle: when it crosses zero.

  Which means that the integration of the sinusoid over one period gives zero: indeed, the two halves have equal area under the curve
  and complementary signs -- therefore their sum cancels out.

  So, assuming that the D.C. offset is constant, the integration over time of the Inverse Fourier Transform gives the value of the D.C. offset.
  (Assuming this integration starts and stops when all partials are crossing zero.)
  Of course, all partials crossing zero at once (all the more twice) is very unlikely (unless the spectrum is fully periodic).
  But a long enough integration will approximate the D.C. offset accurately enough. (Notice how enough relies on enough.)

  /*
    I don't like the word "approximation" either. But noise is a random and unstable phenomenon.
    So we're making sense out of chaos (sounds better?).

    For periodic signals we still get the exact value of the D.C. offset: α[0] (remember?).
  */

  The integration over time of the Inverse Fourier Transform is called the Fourier Transform.

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourier_transform

  How d'you like closure?



